WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.840
Some maps preserve one direction strongly and another only weakly. Recovering an input then requires undoing

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:13.480
a very small scaling, which can amplify noise and rounding. The condition number measures this imbalance

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.140
in a stated norm.

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:21.252
Both values are positive in this example, so the map has full rank. A small minimum

00:00:21.252 --> 00:00:23.347
value marks a weakly preserved direction.

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:30.898
The condition number compares strongest to weakest scaling. A larger ratio means that relative perturbations can

00:00:30.898 --> 00:00:32.217
be strongly amplified.

00:00:32.733 --> 00:00:39.091
What happens to sensitivity as the smallest singular value approaches zero? Pause here and make a

00:00:39.091 --> 00:00:40.283
prediction before continuing.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:48.800
For a full-column-rank matrix, singular values are squared in the Gram matrix. This can worsen numerical

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:54.350
sensitivity. For our starting inputs, Condition number: 20. Gram condition number: 400.

00:00:54.867 --> 00:01:01.871
QR and SVD solve fitting problems without explicitly forming the Gram matrix. This improves the numerical

00:01:01.871 --> 00:01:06.687
route; it does not remove sensitivity inherent in the original data.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.170
A tiny residual does not guarantee accurate recovered parameters in a poorly conditioned problem.

